.TH CANDLED 8 "November 2026" "wincan" "System Administration"
.SH NAME
candled \- serve candles from a collector's binary files, as JSON
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B candled
[\fIOPTION\fR]...
.br
.B candled \-\-gen\-cert
|
.B \-\-fingerprint
|
.BI \-\-authorize " FINGERPRINT\fR [\fB\-\-comment\fR \fITEXT\fR]" 
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B candled
reads the files
.IR DIR/NAME.bin
that a candle collector appends to continuously, aggregates them with rolling windows, and answers
read\-only HTTP requests from
.BR wincan (1)
and anything else that wants candles.  It writes nothing (except, when it is asked to, its own key and the list of clients that are let in); it listens on the loopback address unless told otherwise, speaks TLS, and answers only a client whose key has been authorized (see SECURITY).  Its settings are in
.BR candled.conf (5);
an option overrides the file.
.PP
The files are streams of 30\-byte frames, each
.B {
(a brace), a 28\-byte payload and
.BR } .
A candle payload is
.IR "float O, H, L, C, V, J; uint32 N" ,
one for each 100 milliseconds.  A timestamp marker, once a minute, has N = 0xFFFFFFFF, V = \-1, J = NaN and the Unix time in
seconds in the first four bytes; the next candle begins at that time.  A collector killed in mid\-write leaves a partial frame after
which the frames sit at another offset, and
.B candled
finds them again by the pattern of three frames in a row.  A file that does not end on frames is not listed.
.PP
For each pair the first request reads the newest three days at once and a background thread then reads the rest
of the file, newest part first, into a ring of 600000 one\-minute bars (416 days).  Each later request folds in only what has been
appended.  The timeframes 1m 3m 5m 15m 30m 1h 4h 1d 1w are made from the minute bars on request, aligned to UTC (a week begins on Monday).
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BI \-\-dir " DIR"
the directory of the .bin files (default: \fIcandles\fR in the state directory, /var/lib/candled/candles in a packaged install).
.TP
.BI \-\-db " FILE"
a SQLite database with a table or view
.B candles
(pair, ts in seconds, open, high, low, close, volume) of 1\-minute candles, such as a collector's candle database (pairs are named BTCUSDT there, BTC here).  It is read\-only and is authoritative for every minute it has except the last two minutes of all the data, which come from the ticks in
.BR \-\-dir :
the database may not have them yet, or not in their final form.  Minutes it lacks (it may be filled in batches, once an hour) come from the files.  The pairs it has recent candles for are listed after the pairs of the files.  Volumes from the database are in the database's own unit.  It is asked at most every five seconds, from three hours before its newest row so that rows filled in late are seen.
.TP
.BI \-\-live\-bins " DIR"
a directory of files NAMEUSDT.bin of 80\-byte one\-minute candles (int64 ts, then open, high, low, close, volume and four more doubles, an int and padding), as appended by a websocket daemon for the minutes it has closed.  They are the exchange's own candles: for a closed minute the database comes first, then these, then the ticks of
.BR \-\-dir .
Only the newest days are read.  The price and the age of an answer are those of the freshest source.
.TP
.BI \-\-backfill " DIR"
a second directory of .bin files (for example the spot candles while
.B \-\-dir
holds the perpetual futures'): a minute is taken from
.B \-\-dir
where it has data and otherwise from the backfill, so the charts have the full history from the first day and the primary's data takes over from the moment it starts.  The price and age are the primary's once it has data.  A file that appears in
.B \-\-dir
after the server has started is picked up within ten seconds.  Without it only
.B \-\-dir
is read.
.TP
.BI \-\-port " N"
the TCP port (default 8766).
.TP
.BI \-\-bind " ADDRESS"
.B *
for all addresses; default 127.0.0.1).  An address that is open to a network should have a firewall in front of it.
.TP
.BI \-\-config " FILE" ", \-c"
the configuration file (default: candled.conf in the system configuration directory, if there is one).
.TP
.BR \-\-check\-config ", " \-t
read the configuration, say whether it is good, and exit.
.TP
.B \-\-official\-klines
see OFFICIAL CANDLES AND THE MARK PRICE.
.TP
.B \-\-no\-tls
plain HTTP, without keys: only on a loopback address, unless
.B \-\-insecure
is also given.
.TP
.B \-\-insecure
allow plain HTTP on any address.
.TP
.BI \-\-certificate " FILE\fR, \fB\-\-key\fR \fIFILE"
the server's certificate and private key, PEM (default: server.crt and server.key in the candled directory of the system configuration directory).
.TP
.BI \-\-authorized " FILE"
the file of the fingerprints of the clients that are let in (default: authorized\-clients there).
.TP
.BR \-\-daemon ", " \-D
run in the background; messages go to the system log.
.TP
.BI \-\-pidfile " FILE" ", \-P"
write the process id to the file (and remove it when stopped by SIGTERM or SIGINT).
.TP
.B \-\-gen\-cert
make the server's key (mode 0600) and a self\-signed certificate (an EC P\-256 key, valid ten years), print the certificate's fingerprint, and exit.  Existing files are kept unless
.B \-\-force
is given.
.TP
.B \-\-fingerprint
print the fingerprint of the server's certificate and exit.
.TP
.BI \-\-authorize " FINGERPRINT"
let in the client whose certificate has this SHA\-256 fingerprint (see SECURITY): add it to the authorized file, with
.BI \-\-comment " TEXT"
as a note, and exit.  A running candled picks it up at once.
.TP
.BR \-\-help ", " \-\-version
display help or version information and exit.
.PP
A thread keeps the windows up to date apart from the requests: every loaded pair once a minute, and a pair that has been asked for in the last five seconds every 100 milliseconds, so that a request is answered from memory.  Clients should poll about once a second.
.SH PROTOCOL
The requests and the answers are described in
.BR candle\-protocol (7).
.SH OFFICIAL CANDLES AND THE MARK PRICE
With
.B \-\-official\-klines
and for the pair being watched, a thread fetches KuCoin Futures' own candles of the last three closed minutes every three seconds (again after a second if a fetch failed), and they replace whatever was built from the other sources, whenever they are seen (never for the minute still forming); and it fetches the mark price every second, which the answers carry as
.BR mark .
The price and age of an answer never come from the official candles.
.SH LIMITS
Without markers (the start of an old file, a stalled collector) the file's modification time stands for the end of the data.
Bars older than the last interruption of the collector have their order and shape right but their clock times drift by the length of the gap.
.SH SECURITY
candled has no passwords.  It treats a client as an IRC bouncer treats a client certificate (certfp, SASL EXTERNAL): each client makes a key and a self\-signed certificate of its own, the server is told the SHA\-256 fingerprint of that certificate, and a client whose fingerprint is not on the list gets no answer.  The private key never leaves the client, and traffic is encrypted (TLS 1.2 or later).
.PP
The server's certificate is self\-signed too.  The client pins its fingerprint, so there is no certificate authority to trust or to be fooled.
.TP
.B on the server
.B candled \-\-gen\-cert
once; it prints the server's fingerprint (again with
.BR "candled \-\-fingerprint" ).
The key must be readable by the user that candled runs as.
.TP
.B on the client
the Windows installer of wincan makes the key (in the user's certificate store) and prints its fingerprint, as does
.BR "wincan \-\-fingerprint" .
Any other client can use
.B openssl x509 \-in client.crt \-noout \-fingerprint \-sha256 .
Give the client the server's fingerprint (wincan: \fBserver_pin\fR in wincan.ini).
.TP
.B to let a client in
.B candled \-\-authorize
.I SHA256:\&...
(the digits may be in any of the usual forms: \fBSHA256:ab12...\fR, \fBAB:12:...\fR, or openssl's own output).
.PP
The authorized file has one fingerprint on a line, and may have notes after a
.BR # .
A client that connects without a certificate, or with one that is not on the list, is refused, and the refusal is logged with its fingerprint, which is what to pass to
.BR \-\-authorize .
Plain HTTP, which has no keys, is only for a loopback address unless
.B insecure
is said.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I /etc/candled.conf
the configuration, see
.BR candled.conf (5).
.TP
.I /etc/candled/server.crt, server.key
the server's certificate and private key.
.TP
.I /etc/candled/authorized\-clients
the fingerprints of the clients that are let in.
.TP
.I /var/lib/candled/
the usual place for the candle files.
.PP
(The directories are those of the system configuration and state directories that candled was built with.)
.SH HEAT DATABASE
.B \-\-heat\-db
names a read\-only SQLite database that holds what the heat strip of wincan shows; whatever builds it is not part of this package.  The symbols are the exchange's futures contracts, named like \fBZECUSDTM\fR (BTC is \fBXBTUSDTM\fR).  The tables are:
.TP
.B oi (pair, ts, oi_usd, mark)
the open interest in dollars and the mark price; the newest row of a pair.  Without one there is no answer (404).
.TP
.B bn (pair, ts, global_ls)
the long/short account ratio; the newest row that has one.
.TP
.B book_bands (pair, ts, mid, bid_cov, ask_cov, b1, b2, b5, b10, b20, a1, a2, a5, a10, a20)
the order book's cumulative depth within 1, 2, 5, 10 and 20 per cent of the mid price, bids and asks; the newest row.
.TP
.B heat2 (pair, ts, price, cog, cogn, liq_long, liq_short, src)
the ladder: the liquidation notional of each percentage point from the price, 20 comma\-separated numbers for the longs (below the price) and 20 for the shorts, and the two centres of gravity (a per cent from the price).  A ladder older than ten minutes is not used.
.PP
Without a ladder the notional is modelled in five tiers (1, 2, 5, 10 and 20 per cent) from the open interest and the ratio.  The pressure (pl, ps) is made here: the liquidations up to a distance over the order\-book depth to the same distance.  The leverage mix and the maintenance margin are assumptions, not data.
.SH EXIT STATUS
0 on success (and for \-\-help, \-\-version, \-\-check\-config on a good file); 1 for a failure while running or making a key; 2 for a bad option, a bad configuration, or a refusal to start (plain HTTP on a non\-loopback address, TLS without a certificate).
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR candled.conf (5),
.BR candle\-protocol (7),
.BR wincan (1),
.BR wincan (5)
